<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
     Names the device's ImsService implementations for com.android.phone.

     NOTE: keep double hyphens out of this comment. XML forbids them, and the
     ASCII em-dash style used in the .mk and .bp files of this tree is a hard
     aapt2 error here ("xml parser error: not well-formed").

     PhoneGlobals reads these two strings and hands them to ImsResolver.make()
     as the DEVICE default ImsService (PhoneGlobals.java:554). AOSP declares
     both as empty, so with no overlay ImsResolver has no device configuration
     at all: it discovers the services via their intent-filter, caches them, and
     then binds nothing. `dumpsys phone` showed the whole chain idle:

         Configurations:
           Device:
             EMERGENCY_MMTEL ->        (blank)
             MMTEL ->                  (blank)
             RCS ->                    (blank)
         Cached ImsServices:
           org.codeaurora.ims/.ImsService  featureFromMetadata=false, features: []
         Active controllers:           (empty)

     and the framework logged `registerMmTelCapabilityCallback: registration
     failed, no ImsService available`. That one unbound service accounted for
     the null IMS SMS indication callback, the mIsImsStackUpForSlot flag that
     PowerUpOptimization waits on, and the absent VoLTE plumbing.

     Look for these strings in packages/services/Telephony, NOT in
     frameworks/base: an earlier attempt to find this searched stock's
     framework-res.apk, found nothing, and wrongly concluded the mechanism was
     something else.

     The empty feature list above is a CONSEQUENCE, not a second defect: these
     services declare no feature metadata in their manifests, so ImsResolver has
     to discover their features with a dynamic query, and
     scheduleQueryForFeatures() skips any service that is neither the device
     default nor an active carrier service. Naming them here is what lets the
     query run; the features noted below are what the services then reported for
     themselves.

     One package covers three features: ImsResolver assigns the MMTEL package to
     FEATURE_EMERGENCY_MMTEL as well (ImsResolver.java:668), which is why there
     is no separate emergency string to set.

     Both apks ship from this tree (proprietary-files.txt: ims.apk,
     ImsRcsService.apk), which is why this lives in sm8635-common and not in
     arcfox/overlay-lineage. It is a PRODUCT_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS overlay rather
     than an RRO because com.android.phone is built from source here and is not
     a mainline module, so nothing can replace the APK out of band and take the
     overlay with it. Contrast rro_overlays/WifiOverlay, which must be an RRO
     for exactly that reason.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- org.codeaurora.ims reported {EMERGENCY_MMTEL, MMTEL} on both slots. -->
    <string name="config_ims_mmtel_package" translatable="false">org.codeaurora.ims</string>

    <!-- vendor.qti.imsrcs reported {RCS} on both slots. -->
    <string name="config_ims_rcs_package" translatable="false">vendor.qti.imsrcs</string>
</resources>
